@page "/blocks/seo"

<DocsHeader Title="SEO and sharing" Eyebrow="Website">
    <Lead>What search engines and link previews read: the title, description, canonical address, Open Graph, structured data, a sitemap and robots.txt.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    A website is found through search and shared as links. <code>StilSeo</code> writes the head of a page for
    both, and two builders make the files a site serves at its root. They work in every render mode, and in static
    server rendering they are in the HTML the crawler gets.
</p>

<h2 id="stil-seo">StilSeo</h2>
<p>
    Put one <code>StilSeo</code> on each page, with <code>&lt;HeadOutlet /&gt;</code> in <code>App.razor</code>. It
    replaces <code>PageTitle</code> and <code>HeadContent</code>: use it instead of them, since a page shows only one
    <code>HeadContent</code>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@Page" />
<ul>
    <li><strong>Title</strong> and <strong>SiteName</strong>: the browser's title is "Pricing · My Site"
        (<code>TitleSeparator</code>), a page without a title shows the name, and <code>og:title</code> is the title alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Description</strong>: the meta description and <code>og:description</code>, for search results and previews.</li>
    <li><strong>Canonical</strong>: the page's one address. By default it is the current address without its query and
        fragment, so <code>?utm_source=</code> and the like do not make copies of a page; set it for a page whose query
        matters, such as <code>blog?page=2</code>. It is also <code>og:url</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Image</strong> and <strong>ImageAlt</strong>: the picture of a link preview, made absolute, with the large
        Twitter card. About 1200 × 630 pixels, in PNG or JPEG.</li>
    <li><strong>Type</strong> <code>StilSeoType.Article</code>, with <strong>Published</strong>, <strong>Modified</strong>
        and <strong>Author</strong>, for a post.</li>
    <li><strong>Locale</strong>: <code>og:locale</code>, from the current UI culture by default (sv-SE is <code>sv_SE</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>NoIndex</strong> and <strong>NoFollow</strong>: the robots meta tag, such as for a 404 page or a draft.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="structured-data">Structured data</h2>
<p>
    <code>StilStructuredData</code> builds schema.org objects that search engines read as JSON-LD: the
    <code>Organization</code> behind the site and the <code>WebSite</code> (on the home page), an <code>Article</code>,
    and an <code>FaqPage</code> from questions and answers or from an <code>faq</code> block of a
    <a href="blocks/pages-as-data">page as data</a>. Pass them to <code>StructuredData</code>; use absolute addresses.
    The JSON is escaped, so a text with <code>&lt;/script&gt;</code> in it cannot end the script.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@Article" />

<h2 id="sitemap">Sitemap and robots.txt</h2>
<p>
    <code>StilSitemap</code> builds <code>sitemap.xml</code> from the site's addresses, with when each page last
    changed, and <code>StilRobots</code> builds <code>robots.txt</code>: the paths crawlers should stay out of, and
    where the sitemap is. They are plain classes, so the site maps them to its own endpoints, from its own list of
    pages, such as its posts in a database.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@Endpoints" />
<p>
    A sitemap takes paths relative to the site's address, or absolute addresses, up to 50,000;
    <code>AddPage</code> takes a page as data by its path. Without rules, robots.txt lets every crawler visit
    everything. A value with a line break is refused, so it cannot add a rule.
</p>

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilSeo)" />

@code {
    private const string Page = """
        @page "/pricing"

        <StilSeo Title="Pricing" SiteName="My Site"
                 Description="Free for teams of up to five. Pay for the people who plan."
                 Image="images/share-pricing.png" ImageAlt="The plans side by side" />
        """;

    private const string Article = """
        <StilSeo Title="@post.Title" SiteName="My Site" Description="@post.Excerpt"
                 Type="StilSeoType.Article" Published="post.Date" Author="@post.Author"
                 StructuredData="@([StilStructuredData.Article(post.Title, Navigation.ToAbsoluteUri($"blog/{post.Slug}").AbsoluteUri,
                     published: post.Date, author: post.Author, publisher: "My Site")])" />
        """;

    private const string Endpoints = """
        // Program.cs
        app.MapGet("/sitemap.xml", (HttpRequest request, IBlogService posts) =>
        {
            var sitemap = new StilSitemap($"{request.Scheme}://{request.Host}{request.PathBase}/")
                .Add("/", changeFrequency: StilSitemapFrequency.Weekly, priority: 1)
                .Add("pricing")
                .Add("blog");
            foreach (var post in posts.All())
            {
                sitemap.Add($"blog/{post.Slug}", post.Date);
            }

            return Results.Text(sitemap.ToXml(), "application/xml");
        });

        app.MapGet("/robots.txt", (HttpRequest request) => new StilRobots()
            .Disallow("/admin/")
            .Sitemap($"{request.Scheme}://{request.Host}{request.PathBase}/sitemap.xml")
            .ToString());
        """;
}
